body:
There are four places in structured clone algorithms that can throw on the basis of SharedArrayBuffer-ness (either in whole or as part of a longer condition):

1. _transferable_ is a SharedArrayBuffer in [StructuredSerializeWithTransfer](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#structuredserializewithtransfer) at 2.2 (_transferList_ loop).
2. _value_ is a SharedArrayBuffer and the current settings object’s cross-origin isolated capability is false in [StructuredSerializeInternal](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#structuredserializeinternal) at 13.2.1.
3. _value_ is a SharedArrayBuffer and _forStorage_ is true in in [StructuredSerializeInternal](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#structuredserializeinternal) at 13.2.2.
4. _serialized.[[Type]]_ is "SharedArrayBuffer" but _serialized.[[AgentCluster]]_ isn’t _targetRealm_’s [something something] agent cluster in [StructuredDeserialize](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#structureddeserialize) at 12.

Due to the second, I would expect the following script to reach the catch block:

```js
let { SharedArrayBuffer=sayTheLineBart() } = globalThis;

console.assert(crossOriginIsolated === false, "The case of interest is specific to non-isolated");

try {
  structuredClone(new SharedArrayBuffer(1));
  console.assert(false, "Shouldn’t clone SAB at all without cross-origin isolation.");
} catch (err) {
  console.assert(err.code === DOMException.DATA_CLONE_ERR, "Should throw DataCloneError");
}

function sayTheLineBart() {
  return new WebAssembly.Memory({
    initial: 0,
    maximum: 0,
    shared: 1
  }).buffer.constructor;
}
```

In Firefox it clones successfully, though. Before opening an issue in FF’s tracker, I wanted to find out whether the current spec behavior is “intentional” or if it just happened to arise from reuse of StructuredSerializeWithTransfer. If it’s the latter, then FF’s behavior might be considered a reasonable (if minor) refinement for `structuredClone()`.
